{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2181-05-05 16:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I’m Dr. Lee. I’ll be your doctor during this stay. Can you tell me what brought you to the hospital today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2181-05-05 16:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Hi, Doctor. I’ve been nauseous for two days. After just a few sips of a fizzy lime drink—something I’ve had before—I suddenly felt sick and vomited. Since then, I’ve been dry heaving every 15 minutes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2181-05-05 16:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. Any blood in the vomit? Or abdominal pain?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2181-05-05 16:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No blood in the vomit, but when I made myself vomit a few times, I noticed a little blood on my fingers. I don’t have belly pain, but my stomach muscles are sore from all the heaving."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2181-05-05 16:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. And any fever, chills, or changes in bowel movements?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2181-05-05 16:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I had a low-grade fever, I think. And I’ve had more bowel movements—about five yesterday, some loose, some normal. No blood in the stool, but I felt urgency."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2181-05-05 16:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Got it. You have a history of gastroparesis and a GJ tube. When was your last tube feeding?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2181-05-05 16:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Two nights ago. I skipped it last night because I was too nauseous."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2181-05-05 16:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That makes sense. We’ll start you on IV fluids and anti-nausea meds. We also checked some labs. Your blood work shows a metabolic acidosis, likely from not eating—your body is burning fat for energy, which produces ketones."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2181-05-05 16:39:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, so that’s why I feel so weak? Is that dangerous?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2181-05-05 16:40:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It can be if it gets worse, but we’re treating it with fluids and will restart your tube feeds soon. It should resolve quickly now that we’re supporting you."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2181-05-05 16:41:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that’s a relief. My blood pressure was high in the ER—should I be worried?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2181-05-05 16:42:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your BP was elevated, but it’s likely due to dehydration and stress. It’s already coming down. We’ll keep an eye on it, but no need for new meds right now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2181-05-05 16:43:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Good. And my potassium was low last time—did you check that?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2181-05-05 16:44:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, your potassium was 3.3, which is a bit low, likely from vomiting. We’ve added potassium to your IV fluids to correct that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2181-05-05 16:45:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thanks. I’ve been doing this self-vomiting thing because it helps the nausea, but I know it’s not good."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2181-05-05 16:46:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I understand it gives temporary relief, but it can irritate your esophagus. Your recent EGD showed esophagitis and a small ulcer, so we want to avoid further trauma. That’s probably where the blood on your fingers came from."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2181-05-05 16:47:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, right. So I should stop doing that?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2181-05-05 16:48:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we’ll manage your nausea with IV medications instead—metoclopramide, ondansetron, and a little lorazepam if needed. You’re NPO for now, meaning no food or drink by mouth, to let your stomach rest."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2181-05-05 16:49:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I can do that. What about my tube feeds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2181-05-05 16:50:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll restart them soon, once your nausea is under better control. Nutrition team will guide the timing."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2181-05-06 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your nausea has improved, and labs are trending better. Your acidosis is resolving—your bicarbonate is up to 18 now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2181-05-06 10:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great. Does that mean I can start eating?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2181-05-06 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not yet by mouth, but we’re starting enteral nutrition through your GJ tube today. It’s a concentrated formula to give you calories and help correct the starvation state."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2181-05-06 10:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Perfect. And my stool test came back negative for C. diff, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2181-05-06 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, good catch. Your stool tested negative for C. diff, Salmonella, Shigella, and parasites. No infection found, which supports this being a gastroparesis flare, not infectious colitis."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2181-05-06 10:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m glad it’s not an infection. I was worried about that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2181-05-06 10:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "So are we. Blood cultures were also negative, so no sign of systemic infection."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2181-05-07 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi again. You’ve done well overnight. You tolerated the tube feeds, and your nausea is much better. We’re going to try a clear liquid diet by mouth today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2181-05-07 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Really? That’s great! Can I have water or broth?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2181-05-07 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, small sips of water, broth, or apple juice. We’ll go slow—if you keep it down, we’ll advance it tomorrow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2181-05-07 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my labs? Is the acidosis gone?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2181-05-07 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, your bicarbonate is now 23, and anion gap is normal. The starvation ketosis has resolved with nutrition and fluids."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2181-05-07 09:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s such good news. What about my potassium?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2181-05-07 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your potassium dipped to 3.0 this morning, so we gave a supplement. It’s now 4.5 and stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2181-05-08 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve made great progress. You’ve kept down clear liquids, your vitals are stable, and labs are normal. We’re planning to discharge you today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2181-05-08 08:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s wonderful. Are my medications the same?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2181-05-08 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we’re sending you home on your usual meds: lansoprazole, levothyroxine, citalopram, and your anti-nausea meds—metoclopramide four times a day now, and ondansetron or lorazepam as needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2181-05-08 08:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll make sure to take the metoclopramide regularly. Any diet advice?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2181-05-08 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Continue with clear liquids today, then advance slowly to soft foods. Avoid fizzy drinks and dairy for now—they can trigger symptoms. Restart full tube feeds tonight if tolerated."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2181-05-08 08:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. And follow-up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2181-05-08 08:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Follow up with your PCP in a few days. They’ll monitor your progress and review any pending stool tests, though they’re likely negative."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2181-05-08 08:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you, Doctor. I feel so much better already."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2181-05-08 08:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Keep using your tube for nutrition, take your meds, and call if nausea returns or you can’t keep liquids down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2181-05-08 08:09:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thanks for taking such good care of me."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2181-05-05 16:26:00",
    "admission_end": "2181-05-08 14:48:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A woman with a history of gastroparesis following Nissen fundoplication was admitted for a two-day episode of nausea, vomiting, and increased bowel movements triggered by a fizzy drink. She presented with metabolic acidosis, hypokalemia, and transient hypertension, all of which resolved with IV fluids, electrolyte repletion, and bowel rest. She was maintained NPO initially, treated with IV antiemetics, and enteral nutrition was restarted on hospital day 3. Her nausea improved, she tolerated a clear liquid diet, and was discharged home in stable condition. No infectious cause was found on stool or blood cultures. Discharge instructions included resuming home medications, advancing diet gradually, and follow-up with her primary care provider.",
    "problems": [
      "Gastroparesis",
      "Acidosis",
      "Hyperosmolality and/or hypernatremia",
      "Hypopotassemia",
      "Esophagitis, unspecified",
      "Personal history of malignant neoplasm of breast",
      "Unspecified acquired hypothyroidism",
      "Dysthymic disorder",
      "Irritable bowel syndrome",
      "Effects of hunger",
      "Status of other artificial opening of gastrointestinal tract"
    ]
  }
}
